# ABSTRACT

Hydrocarbons separation in petrochemical industries is a key, energy-consuming stage in the manufacture of high-quality added-value products—hence the need for more efficient materials and environmentally friendly methodologies to improve this process. In this context, we have studied the effect of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) pore functionalization in hexane isomers separation, isolating the robust isoreticular zinc(II) bipyrazolates \(Z\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{B P Z})\), showing no pore decoration, \(Z\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{M e}_{2}\mathsf{B P Z})\), the pores of which are decorated with apolar methyl groups, and \(Z\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{B P Z}(\mathsf{N H}_{2})_{2})\), the spacers of which possess polar Lewis-basic functions \((\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B P Z}=1H,1\,H{-}4,4^{\prime}\) -bipyrazole, \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{M e}_{2}\mathsf{B P Z}=3,3^{\prime}\) -dimethyl- \(\cdot1H,1\,H{-}4,4^{\prime}\) -bipyrazole, \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B P Z}(\mathsf{N H}_{2})_{2}=3,5\) -diamino\(1H,1\,H{-}4,4^{\prime}\) -bipyrazole; \(\mathsf{D M F}\,=\) dimethylformamide). After characterizing \(Z\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{B P Z}(\mathsf{N H}_{2})_{2})\) as per its crystal structure and thermal behaviour, and all the three MOFs as per their textural properties, we investigated, from the experimental and computational points of view, the impact of the square one-dimensional channels decoration on the separation of the hexane isomers, demonstrating the relevance of pore constrictions in the resolution of the title alkanes mixture.

# KEYWORDS

adsorption, molecular sieving, nitrogen-donor ligands, gas separation, powder diffraction, variable-temperature pulse gas chromatography

# 1 Introduction

Hydrocarbons separation in petrochemical industries is a crucial, energy-consuming step toward the manufacture of high-quality added-value products such as gasoline, polymers, plastics [1]. As a representative example, the efficiency of internal combustion engines is related to the reactivity of the hydrocarbons comprised in gasoline. An indirect assessment of gasoline grade is the so-called research octane number (RON) [2]. Linear hydrocarbons, reacting faster than branched ones, possess low RON. As far as the five \(\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{14}\) isomers \(\mathrm{HEX}=\) n-hexane, \(2\mathrm{MP}=2\) -methylpentane, \(3\mathrm{MP}=3\) -methylpentane, \(2,2\mathrm{DMB}=2,2\) -dimethylbutane, \(2,3\mathrm{DMB}=2,3\) -dimethylbutane) are concerned [3], the RON increases from the value of 30 of HEX to those of 74 and 75 of the monobranched 2MP and 3MP, respectively, and those of 94 and 105 of the dibranched 2,2DMB and 2,3DMB, respectively. For commercial purposes, a mixture containing only \(\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{14}\) dibranched isomers is thus more appealing than one containing also the monobranched and linear isomers, due to its higher final RON.

On a large scale, after catalytic isomerization reactions, \(\mathrm{C}_{6}\)

isomers are typically separated by fractional distillation, an expensive process limited by the similar physico-chemical properties of the alkanes to be separated (e.g. boiling points in the range 323–341 K). Separation by selective adsorption is a promising alternative. Presently, low RON paraffines are separated from high RON ones through the Total Isomerization Process \((\mathrm{TIP}^{\mathrm{TM}})\) from UOP [1, 4, 5], or the Ipsorb™ process from Axens [6, 7]. These processes recur to zeolite 5A, which is capable of separating linear hydrocarbons from mono- and dibranched ones, granting a final RON of 86. A better separation performance can be obtained by combining zeolite 5A with zeolite \(\upbeta\) in a dual-layer pressure swing adsorption bed [8], yielding a RON enhancement up to 92. Nonetheless, due to their similar polarizability and inert behaviour, an efficient separation of mono- from dibranched hexane isomers with selectivity and capacity higher than those of zeolite 5A is still a challenge.

